# Opm CLI Reference

File: opm/docs/cli.oot

This document is the user-facing reference for the opm command set. opm is the openOODA package manager. It runs as a subcommand of the ooda toolchain. After install, the entry point is `ooda opm <subcommand>`.

## 1. Top-Level Command

The dispatcher lives at `cli/opm/main.oo`. It parses `argv[0]` as the subcommand and routes the rest of `argv` plus the four root capability tokens (`&FsReadCap`, `&FsWriteCap`, `&ProcessCap`, `&EnvCap`) to the right `run_ooda_pm_*` handler. An unknown subcommand prints the help line and exits 2.

The full form is `ooda opm <subcommand> [args]`. Every subcommand is fail-closed. A bad name, a yanked version, a sha mismatch, a missing lock file, or a cap outside the allow-list is an error, not a warning.

## 2. Subcommand Index

opm exposes 19 user-facing subcommand handlers. Each one maps to a `cli/opm/cmd_*.oo` file.

| Subcommand | Purpose | Handler file |
|---|---|---|
| `add` | Resolve and install one or more packages | `cli/opm/cmd_add.oo` |
| `audit` | Walk the lock against yank, advisory, and pins | `cli/opm/cmd_audit.oo` |
| `bot` | Run the package bot script | `cli/opm/cmd_bot.oo` |
| `cat` | Print a catalog row | `cli/opm/cmd_cat.oo` |
| `git` | Resolve a local git pin to a commit | `cli/opm/cmd_git.oo` |
| `ingest` | Lock a file-backed recipe (dir, git, deb, rpm, appimage) | `cli/opm/cmd_ingest.oo` |
| `init` | Write a starter `ooda.pkg` manifest | `cli/opm/cmd_init.oo` |
| `io` | File and stream helpers | `cli/opm/cmd_io.oo` |
| `launch` | Re-verify and optionally run a pinned payload | `cli/opm/cmd_launch.oo` |
| `more` | Print the full help text | `cli/opm/cmd_more.oo` |
| `ops` | The lifecycle group (remove, update, outdated, why) | `cli/opm/cmd_ops.oo` |
| `pack` | Build an offline pack from the lock | `cli/opm/cmd_pack.oo` |
| `pick` | Pick the best version from a range | `cli/opm/cmd_pick.oo` |
| `pkg` | Inspect or print the current `ooda.pkg` | `cli/opm/cmd_pkg.oo` |
| `publish` | Write a manifest + payload to a local registry tree | `cli/opm/cmd_publish.oo` |
| `reg` | Configure a registry URL (alias of `regs`) | `cli/opm/cmd_reg.oo` |
| `regs` | Walk and list configured registries | `cli/opm/cmd_regs.oo` |
| `search` | Search every added HTTPS store by substring | `cli/opm/cmd_search.oo` |
| `tree` | Print a dep tree, the file vendor, or prune CAS | `cli/opm/cmd_tree.oo` |

## 3. Aliases and Short Forms

The dispatcher maps a few short names to the same handler. The aliases are:

- `reg`, `registry` → `regs`
- `sbom` → `audit`
- `remove`, `update`, `outdated`, `why` → `ops` (with a sub-arg)
- `vendor`, `clean` → `tree` (with a sub-arg)

Use the long form in scripts. The aliases exist for interactive shells.

## 4. Common Flags

Every subcommand supports a small set of common flags:

- `--json` — print a machine object instead of text. Every subcommand that produces output supports this.
- `--vendor <path>` — point at a non-stock file vendor (a directory that has a `catalog`, `index`, `yank`, and `advisory` file).
- `--from <dir>` — for `add` and `ingest`, install from a local directory recipe instead of the catalog.

Subcommand-specific flags are listed below.

## 5. Subcommand Reference

### 5.1 `ooda opm add <name> [--vendor path] [--from dir] [--pubgrub] [--json]`

Resolve a named package or all roots in `ooda.pkg`, then write to CAS, the file vendor, and a v2 lock. Refuses bad names, yanks, license misses, extra caps, sha drift, and missing pins. `--pubgrub` opts into the backtracking resolver (default is single-pass with the standard selector).

### 5.2 `ooda opm audit [--json]` (alias: `sbom`)

Read `.ooda_modules/lock`, the vendor `yank` and `advisory` files, and the std/oodac pins. Print any hit. With no hits, print `ooda audit: ok`. The `sbom` form prints a CycloneDX-lite object.

### 5.3 `ooda opm bot [args]`

Run the package bot. The bot is a small script the user can ship beside their package. It runs with the same caps as `add` and prints structured output.

### 5.4 `ooda opm cat <name>`

Print the catalog row for a name: name, version, caps, deps, sha. Used to debug resolution.

### 5.5 `ooda opm git <name>`

Resolve a local git pin to its commit hash. Used by `ingest` to verify that a `git:name#commit` line has a matching local commit file.

### 5.6 `ooda opm ingest --from <dir> [--json]`

Read a dir, local git, or hashed blob recipe and lock the payload. Refuses HTTP, bad names, extra caps, yanks, and license misses. Verifies `sha256` and the local git commit. Writes CAS, an ingest wrapper, and a v2 lock.

### 5.7 `ooda opm init [name] [--json]`

Write a starter `ooda.pkg` manifest with name, version `0.1.0`, `min_pin`, empty `deps`, two starter caps, an MIT license, and empty signature fields. Refuses to overwrite an existing `ooda.pkg`.

### 5.8 `ooda opm io [args]`

File and stream helpers used by other subcommands. Not for direct end-user use; listed for completeness.

### 5.9 `ooda opm launch <name> [--exec]`

Re-hash the CAS blob. Refuse yank, extra caps, or sha drift. Without `--exec`, print the path to the pinned payload. With `--exec`, run the pinned `.oo` after the gate passes. If `OODA_UNTRUSTED` is set, exec is refused even with the flag.

### 5.10 `ooda opm more`

Print the full help text with every subcommand listed.

### 5.11 `ooda opm ops <subaction> [args]`

The lifecycle group. Subactions are:

- `remove <name>` — drop a package from the lock and CAS.
- `update [name]` — re-resolve and refresh the lock.
- `outdated` — list packages with a newer compatible version.
- `why <name>` — print why a package is in the lock (its parents).

### 5.12 `ooda opm pack [name] [--json]`

Build an offline pack. The default output dir is `.ooda_pack`. The pack contains the lock, a signed index, and one directory per locked package with its `manifest` and payload. No tar, no network.

### 5.13 `ooda opm pick <name> <range>`

Pick the highest version of a name that satisfies a range. Used by tools that want to test resolve without installing.

### 5.14 `ooda opm pkg [--json]`

Inspect or print the current `ooda.pkg`. Reads name, version, deps, caps, license, payload, and sig.

### 5.15 `ooda opm publish [--to dir] [--json]`

Write the local `ooda.pkg` manifest and the payload to a registry tree. The default is `.ooda-cache/registry`. `--to <dir>` overrides. Creates `<root>/<name>/<version>/`, writes `ooda.pkg`, the payload, and a signed `index`. HMAC signs the index. No network. The user can `rsync` the tree to a real server.

### 5.16 `ooda opm reg <url>`, `ooda opm registry <url>`

Add or list a registry URL. Alias of `regs`. Only HTTPS hosts are accepted. The default is `https://registry.openooda.org`.

### 5.17 `ooda opm regs`

Walk the configured store URLs and print their `name|version|caps|deps|sha` rows.

### 5.18 `ooda opm search <query>`

Fetch each catalog and print names that match the query (a substring of the package name). Empty query lists all.

### 5.19 `ooda opm tree [--json]`, `ooda opm vendor`, `ooda opm clean`

- `tree` walks the catalog deps with indent and prints the resolved tree. `--json` emits nodes.
- `vendor` lists the file vendor: every name and version in `vendor/`.
- `clean` removes CAS dirs whose sha is not in the current lock.

## 6. Capability Requirements

Every opm subcommand takes only the granular capability it needs. The minimum is one of `&FsReadCap`, `&FsWriteCap`, `&NetCap`, `&HttpCap`, or `&ProcessCap`. opm never requires broad caps (`&FsCap`, `&SysCap`). The dispatcher itself is the only place that takes `&EnvCap` (used to read `OODA_UNTRUSTED` and `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`).

## 7. Exit Codes

- `0` — success.
- `1` — manifest or payload missing.
- `2` — bad name, yank, license, cap, sha, sig, or pin miss.

The exact code per handler is listed in `cli/opm/cmd_*.oo`. Scripts should treat any non-zero as a hard fail.
